    a: "<b>Flumazenil:</b> Competitive benzodiazepine receptor antagonist<br><b>Dose:</b> 0.2 mg IV over 30 seconds; repeat 0.2 mg q1 min; max 1 mg<br><b>Duration:</b> <b>~20 min</b> — much shorter than most benzodiazepines → re-sedation is likely<br><b>Cautions and contraindications:</b><ul><li><b>Mixed overdose (BZD + TCA or other epileptogenic drugs)</b> — flumazenil can unmask seizures previously suppressed by BZD; may precipitate status epilepticus</li><li><b>Chronic BZD use / BZD dependence</b> — precipitates severe withdrawal including refractory seizures</li><li>Do NOT use routinely in undifferentiated coma — risk exceeds benefit</li><li>Do NOT use in known seizure disorder on chronic BZD</li></ul><b>Clinical pearl:</b> Flumazenil is most safely used in <b>procedural sedation reversal</b> where benzodiazepine was given as the only agent and the patient has no BZD dependence.<br><b>Bottom line:</b> If in doubt, supportive care (airway management) is safer than flumazenil in mixed or chronic BZD overdose."
