    q: "A 22-year-old presents with acetaminophen overdose 6 hours ago. Serum acetaminophen level is above the treatment line on the nomogram. What is the antidote and timing of treatment?",
    a: "<b>Acetaminophen (APAP) overdose management:</b><br><b>Antidote: N-acetylcysteine (NAC)</b><br><b>Mechanism:</b> Replenishes glutathione, conjugates NAPQI (toxic metabolite from CYP2E1)<br><b>Timing and efficacy:</b><ul><li>Most effective within <b>8 hours</b> of ingestion — near 100% hepatoprotection if started within 8h</li><li>Still beneficial up to 24h or beyond in severe toxicity</li><li>Start immediately if APAP level is above the <b>Rumack-Matthew nomogram treatment line</b> (plotted at 4h post-ingestion)</li></ul><b>Route and dosing:</b><ul><li><b>IV NAC (preferred for severe/uncertain cases):</b> 150 mg/kg in 200 mL D5W over 60 min, then 50 mg/kg over 4h, then 100 mg/kg over 16h</li><li><b>Oral NAC:</b> 140 mg/kg loading, then 70 mg/kg q4h × 17 doses (GI intolerance common)</li></ul><b>Monitoring:</b><ul><li>LFTs, INR, creatinine q12–24h</li><li>If King's College Criteria met → transplant evaluation</li></ul>"
