    a: "<b>Salicylate toxicity:</b><br><b>Classic presentation:</b><ul><li><b>Tinnitus</b> (earliest sign)</li><li><b>Hyperventilation</b> (respiratory alkalosis — direct stimulation of respiratory center)</li><li><b>Metabolic high-anion gap acidosis</b> (uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation)</li><li>Mixed disorder: <b>respiratory alkalosis + metabolic acidosis</b> (classic combination)</li><li>Nausea, vomiting, altered mental status, hyperthermia, diaphoresis</li></ul><b>Dangerous when:</b> Acidemia shifts salicylate into CNS (un-ionized form crosses blood-brain barrier)<br><b>Treatment:</b><ul><li><b>IV sodium bicarbonate</b> (urinary alkalinization) — raises urine pH to &gt;7.5, ionizes salicylate in urine, traps it for excretion; also prevents CNS entry</li><li>IV fluids, glucose (glucose even if normoglycemic — CNS glucose depletion)</li><li>Potassium replacement (required for effective urinary alkalinization)</li><li><b>Hemodialysis</b> for: altered mental status, severe acidosis, pulmonary edema, renal failure, or salicylate &gt;100 mg/dL</li></ul>"
