    q: "What is the mechanism and clinical use of metformin, and what are its contraindications and precautions regarding contrast media?",
    a: "<b>Metformin (biguanide):</b><br><b>Mechanism:</b> Activates AMPK → decreases hepatic gluconeogenesis (primary); also improves peripheral insulin sensitivity, reduces GI glucose absorption<br><b>Clinical advantages:</b><ul><li>No weight gain (often slight weight loss)</li><li>No hypoglycemia as monotherapy</li><li>CV benefit (UKPDS): mortality reduction in overweight T2DM</li><li>Inexpensive, well-studied</li></ul><b>Contraindications:</b><ul><li><b>eGFR &lt;30 mL/min/1.73m²</b> — absolute contraindication (lactic acidosis risk)</li><li>Caution: eGFR 30–45 (do not initiate; may continue at lower dose if already on)</li><li>Acute/decompensated HF, hepatic failure, excessive alcohol use</li></ul><b>Contrast media precaution:</b><ul><li>Hold metformin <b>before or at time of IV iodinated contrast</b> if eGFR &lt;60 or any AKI risk factor</li><li>Restart 48h after contrast if renal function stable</li><li>Reason: contrast-induced nephropathy → impaired metformin excretion → lactic acidosis</li></ul>"
