    a: "<b>SSRI-induced hyponatremia — SIADH mechanism:</b><br><b>Mechanism:</b><ul><li>SSRIs increase serotonin → stimulates ADH (AVP) release → water retention → dilutional hyponatremia</li><li>Classified as SIADH (Syndrome of Inappropriate ADH secretion)</li></ul><b>SIADH diagnostic criteria:</b><ul><li>Serum Na &lt;136 mEq/L + serum osmolality &lt;275 mOsm/kg</li><li><b>Urine osmolality &gt;100 mOsm/kg</b> (kidney inappropriately concentrating urine)</li><li><b>Urine Na &gt;40 mEq/L</b> (sodium not retained)</li><li>Euvolemic (no edema, no orthostasis)</li><li>Normal thyroid, adrenal function</li></ul><b>Other SIADH causes:</b><ul><li>CNS disorders, pulmonary disease (TB, lung cancer — SCLC most common tumor), medications (thiazides, carbamazepine, cyclophosphamide, desmopressin), postoperative pain</li></ul><b>Management:</b><ul><li>Mild-moderate SIADH: <b>fluid restriction 1–1.5 L/day</b></li><li>If persistent: <b>salt tablets ± loop diuretics</b></li><li>Tolvaptan/conivaptan (V2 receptor antagonists) for resistant cases — avoid in liver disease</li><li>Stop offending drug if possible</li></ul>"
