    a: "<b>Dexamethasone in bacterial meningitis:</b><br><b>Mechanism:</b> Suppresses cytokine release (TNF, IL-1) → reduces neuroinflammation and blood-brain barrier disruption<br><b>Indication:</b> Adults with suspected or confirmed bacterial meningitis (Streptococcus pneumoniae)<br><b>Dosing and timing:</b><ul><li><b>Dexamethasone 0.15 mg/kg IV q6h × 4 days</b></li><li>Give <b>15–20 minutes BEFORE or concurrent with first dose of antibiotic</b></li><li>If antibiotics already given, steroids provide minimal benefit</li></ul><b>Evidence:</b><ul><li>Reduces mortality and neurological sequelae in pneumococcal meningitis (European trial)</li><li>Benefit less clear for other organisms (Listeria, Neisseria) but not harmful</li></ul><b>Dexamethasone in cerebral edema (vasogenic):</b><ul><li>Brain tumor-associated edema: dexamethasone 4–8 mg PO/IV q6–12h (very effective for tumor-related edema)</li><li><b>NOT indicated</b> for edema from ischemic stroke (worsens outcome) or traumatic brain injury</li></ul><b>Dexamethasone equivalency:</b> 4 mg dexamethasone = 20 mg prednisone (relative anti-inflammatory potency)"
